  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1012 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nA stronger push of cold air remains on deck for Thursday, helping\nto increase the northerly winds again, particularly across far\nnortheast Oklahoma and into northwest Arkansas. This colder air\nwill lead to widespread frost potential Thursday night into Friday\nmorning across northeast Oklahoma and northwest Arkansas, along \nwith patchy frost that extends as far south as McAlester, \nWilburton and Poteau. While frost/freeze headlines will not be \nissued at the moment, a frost mention has been added to the\nforecast. Highs on Friday may struggle to reach 50 degrees in\nparts of northwest Arkansas, with afternoon temperatures around 10\ndegrees below seasonal normals despite plenty of sun. Friday night\nand Saturday morning may actually be the coolest morning for most\nareas, with another round of frost extending into southeast\nOklahoma now mentioned in the forecast. \n\nSoutheasterly winds make a return on Saturday ahead of another\nstorm system. Increased cloudiness and the easterly wind component\nwill lead to only a modest warm up for most areas, with\ntemperatures overall remaining below normal. Mid level moisture\nassociated with the storm system looks to really begin to increase\nSaturday night into Sunday morning, leading to a corresponding\nincrease in the potential for rain after midnight. The amount of\ndry air in the low levels may lead to a delayed onset in\nmeasurable rain initially. Rain chances will peak during the day\nSunday and decrease from west to east Sunday night into early\nMonday as the storm system quickly shifts east. The rainy daytime\nconditions on Sunday will keep temperatures chilly areawide, with\ngusty winds making things feel even worse. Temperatures begin to \nwarm early to mid week ahead of another system to the west. This \nsystem will bring the next chance of rain mid week.",
